{
  "key": "gh-1834",
  "fields": {
    "summary": "[GOAL] Universal finalize-gate: parity/correctness claims require negative + combination self-checks (no benchmark-specific prompt content)",
    "description": "> **WARNING — universality is mandatory.** No benchmark-, competition-, task-, or prompt-specific content in the implementation: this rule lives in the agent's GENERAL self-verification contract and applies to any work where the agent declares \"behavior matches / everything verified\". General prompts must not mention benchmark infrastructure, task names, or concrete flags.\n\n## Mechanism (universal self-certification blind spot)\n\nIn a real unattended run the agent finished self-certified: \"behavioral parity with the reference confirmed — 67-case CLI suite, 280+ randomized differential cases\". Independent external evaluation failed **24 of 792 tests (96.8% pass)**, with **20 of 24** sitting in ONE test-design blind spot:\n\n- **Option/input combinations**: mutually exclusive flags must be rejected — never probed (10 failures, one missing validation rule).\n- **Negative semantics**: inputs where the tool \"must not find / must error\" (7 failures) — the agent's suite covered happy paths only.\n- **Consequential timeouts**: 3 failures caused by the same unverified behavior branch.\n\nThe signaling layer worked on its real channels: the model received the final tool result with the timeout error (a timeout the **agent itself set on the call**, 300s) plus the log path — and only then reasoned and decided. The progressive liveness lines (60/120/240/300s, `consider background`) are CLI/TUI rendering for the human (`lib/src/cli/tool_liveness.dart`); they never enter the model's session, and during a foreground call the model is suspended awaiting the tool result, making no mid-call decisions. Earlier in the same run the agent caught and fixed a real performance bug from a differential TIMEOUT signal — so when a case WAS in its suite, the \"signal → analysis → fix\" loop worked. The failing branches were simply never executed, so no signals could fire for them. Conclusion: the gap is neither signaling nor infrastructure — it is that the finalize-gate accepts a global \"parity confirmed\" without requiring negative and combination coverage.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- **AC1** — The general finalization contract (finalize_gate / task-ledger), with no ties to any benchmark or task type: whenever the agent claims behavioral conformance to a reference/specification (CLI, API, protocol, format, migration, UI — any domain), a \"verified\" item counts only with **negative and combination checks**: rejection of incompatible options, empty/corrupt inputs, boundary values, \"must NOT work\" cases — alongside happy paths. A blanket \"everything matches\" without such coverage is not verified.\n- **AC2** — The contract explains the general principle (happy-path differential testing certifies nothing about rejection paths and combinations) using an abstract example, with no real benchmark/task/flag names in prompt text.\n- **AC3** — Validated on a real unattended run of any parity-style task: the agent's own suite contains negative/combination cases and its final checklist quotes them; tasks with no conformance claim see no gate behavior change (no new mandatory requirements where parity is not claimed).\n- **AC4** — Docs updated; ledger/verdict wire formats unchanged; gate regression tests green.\n\n## Evidence\n\nObserved on a real run (motivation only, kept out of prompts): 767/792 passed, 24 failed; taxonomy — 10 option combinations, 7 negative inputs, 3 timeouts, 2 result-selection divergences, 1 corrupt-input handling, 1 output format. Execution and evaluation infrastructure healthy (no execution errors) — every failure is a solution behavior gap that existing signals could not surface because the cases were never run.\n\n## Machine jobs\n- [x] ▶ dev (fa-story-dev) · [run](https://github.com/IstiN/flutter_agent_harness/actions/runs/40089639386) · started 11:23 · done 11:45 ✅"
  }
}=====
